The basic rule is:

VERY

Used with:

Adjective of positive degree
Present participle (-ing)
MUCH

Used with:

Adjective of comparative degree
Past participle (V3)


1. VERY + Positive Degree Adjective

A positive degree adjective is the normal/basic form:

good
beautiful
intelligent
tall
difficult
happy

So:

She is very good at painting. ✅

He is very intelligent. ✅

The question is very difficult. ✅

❌ Don't use MUCH with a normal positive adjective in this pattern:

❌ She is much good.

✅ She is very good.


2. MUCH + Comparative Degree

Comparative adjectives generally have:

better
bigger
taller
more beautiful
more intelligent
stronger

Use much to intensify the comparative.

He is much smarter than his brother. ✅

She is much better than me. ✅

This book is much more interesting than that one. ✅

So:

much + comparative

Compare:

He is very smart. ✅
He is much smarter than his brother. ✅

very → positive

much → comparative


3. VERY + Present Participle

A present participle is generally:

V + ing

Examples:

crying
running
smiling
interesting
boring
exciting

According to this rule, use very with a present participle when it is functioning as an adjective.

The movie is very interesting. ✅

She is very charming. ✅

He is very annoying. ✅

It was very exciting. ✅

Why?

Words like interesting, exciting, annoying are present participles functioning adjectivally.

So:

very + present participle

4. MUCH + Past Participle

Use much with a past participle (V3) when it has an adjectival/passive sense.

Examples:

He was much disappointed. ✅

She was much surprised. ✅

I was much confused. ✅

He is much admired by everyone. ✅

So:

much + past participle
